##" Every US public library system and branch in the IMLS Public Libraries Survey: FY2023 in detail, plus every system FY2018 - FY2024 (use, spend, e-content and print budgets) and US Census county population 2010 - 2025, so library use and spend can be read per county resident and against population growth. Built to answer buyer questions: which libraries an e-book vendor should call and how big that opportunity is (econtent_prospects -> econtent_opportunity_headline: $17.2M a year across 1,064 libraries, top 25 hold $10.1M; then libraries_to_pitch_digital_content; high_demand_low_spend is a separate second list, never added to the headline), how big the library e-content market is (library_years -> econtent_market_by_year: $724M in FY2024; not econtent_prospects, which covers benchmarked systems only), which fast-growing counties underfund their libraries and what closing the gap costs (county_spend_gap -> fast_growing_counties_underfunding_libraries), value per dollar by system size (library_years -> systems_by_size_value_per_dollar) and recovery since 2019 (library_years -> library_use_recovery_since_2019). Defaults to North Carolina; pick any state. Sources: IMLS Public Libraries Survey FY2018 - FY2024 (public-use files, no licence required) and US Census county population estimates (Vintage 2025 and 2020), downloaded 2026-10-06; FY2024 totals checked against IMLS's own file on 2026-10-07. FY2024 caveat: 2,791 systems have no borrowing figure, so per-person rates use reporting systems only.
## dataset { title="US Public Libraries (FY2018 - FY2024)" }

import { library_systems, library_branches, library_years, county_population, county_libraries, econtent_candidates, econtent_prospects, county_spend_gap, STATE, SIZE_BAND } from 'libraries.malloy'

export { library_systems, library_branches, library_years, county_population, county_libraries, econtent_candidates, econtent_prospects, county_spend_gap, STATE, SIZE_BAND }
